// #2614 (epic #1844), sibling of #2613/#3603's tests/bit/schedmainstack.bit.
// That file proves `main`'s dedicated 8 MiB stack (`schedMainStackBytes`,
// found via `schedRegisterBigStack`); this one proves the OTHER path every
// other task in the process uses: the masked-identity granule,
// `schedStackBytes` (256 KiB, `runtime/sched/sched.bit`). `main` moved off
// masking entirely in #2613, so this is the only case left that exercises
// `schedTaskOnStack(sp & ~(schedStackBytes - 1))` at real depth.
//
// THE MECHANISM THIS PROTECTS. Identity is not carried by a thread-local; it
// is derived by masking the live stack pointer down to the `schedStackBytes`
// boundary and reading a tag word at the base (`sched.bit`'s own header on
// `schedTaskOnStack`). That makes it an ALIGNMENT-DERIVED lookup: it depends
// on `schedTaskInit`'s `stackBase` being an exact multiple of
// `schedStackBytes`, and on no call chain inside the task ever exceeding the
// granule (masking a stack pointer from anywhere inside a task must land on
// THAT task's own base, which is what caps a masked-identity stack at exactly
// this size). A shrunk granule, a misaligned base, or a call chain that
// crosses out of the granule all fail the SAME way: silently, resolving to
// the wrong task or to none, never a fault at the mask itself.
//
// A channel send/receive is the discriminator, not incidental plumbing: it is
// the operation that reaches `schedCurrentTask()` (to find the task's own
// select/error scratch words), so it is what actually exercises identity
// resolution at depth — same reasoning as schedmainstack.bit. A BROKEN
// identity does not panic cleanly here — it resolves to task 0, which the
// runtime treats as fatal (rootExit(70)), so this case's exit code IS the
// assertion: 0 only if `schedCurrentTask()` named the spawned task correctly
// from 2000 frames down its own 256 KiB stack.
//
// DEPTH IS FIXED AT 2000 FRAMES (do not raise): 256 KiB holds roughly 3400
// frames of this shape on arm64, so 2000 leaves comfortable headroom below
// the cliff — the same "deliberately far from the edge" posture
// ../stress/greenstackdepth documents for the pre-#2613 granule, without
// re-deriving the exact byte budget here (this function's frame carries an
// extra `chan<int>` parameter over that fixture's single `int`, so the two
// per-frame costs are not the same number and should not be compared
// directly).
const DEPTH: int = 2000

fn down(n: int, c: chan<int>) {
  if (n <= 0) {
    c <- 1
    return
  }
  down(n - 1, c)
}

fn worker(c: chan<int>) {
  down(DEPTH, c)
}

fn main() {
  let c = chan<int>(1)
  spawn worker(c)
  let v = <-c
  if (v != 1) {
    print("wrong: ${v}\n")
    return
  }
  print("ok\n")
}
